<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Murche-Khort</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1729">1729</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Murchakhurt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Murche-Khort was fought in November 1729 north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, when Nader, then general of the Safavid claimant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-ii">Tahmasp II</a>, defeated the Afghan army of the Hotaki ruler Ashraf. Following Nader's victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damghan">Damghan</a> weeks earlier, it forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> to abandon Isfahan, ending their seven-year occupation of the Safavid capital and allowing Tahmasp II to return there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-murche-khort.jpg" alt="Battle of Murche-Khort" width="320" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: نامشخص. احتمالاً محمد علی بیک نقاشباشی یا علی بن محمد زمان یا علی اشرف. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jahangusha-ye_Naderi_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After his defeat by Nader in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashraf-hotak">Ashraf Hotak</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and had more than 3,000 Iranian aristocrats and clergymen massacred, so that no leaders would be left to raise the city against him once he marched out to meet Nader. He had also failed to stop Nader's advance at the Khwar pass, where his ambush was discovered and surrounded. Nader, meanwhile, wrote to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> demanding that they withdraw from the Safavid lands they had gained from Ashraf by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>.</p><p>Seeing the threat of a resurgent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> on their eastern border, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> answered Ashraf's appeals for help with guns and artillerymen. On 31 October 1729, his firepower much increased, Ashraf marched out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and took up a defensible position near Murche-Khort, about 55 kilometres north of the city. The Swedish traveller Jean Otter later wrote that Ashraf feared being shut up in a conquered city whose inhabitants might rise against him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Nader's army came into view on 13 November 1729. He tried to draw Ashraf out of his entrenchments, first with artillery fire and then by marching around him towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, but neither ruse worked. Ashraf had adopted something of his enemy's methods: his artillery stood in a ring around a long line of entrenched infantry in the centre, a pivot around which his cavalry could manoeuvre.</p><p>Nader drew up his Jazāyerchi musketeers opposite the Afghan line, sent cavalry under Haj Beg Afshar to threaten the Afghan right and kept the rest in reserve. The musketeers advanced to the trenches, fired volleys at close range and closed with swords. Repeated Afghan cavalry charges against their flank and rear were thrown back by the reserve, and the Afghan centre finally broke.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Ashraf fled so fast that he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> the same afternoon. He loaded what valuables he could onto pack animals and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> at dawn, taking a few Safavid princesses with him. The captured Ottoman artillerymen were treated with mercy and allowed to go home. The victory opened the road to Isfahan and restored Safavid rule for a few years, until Nader overthrew it in 1736, made himself Shah and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid dynasty</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Murche-Khort” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He defeated the Ghilzai Afghans at Damghan and <b>Murche-Khort</b> and expelled them from Isfahan in 1729, freeing the Safavid capital after seven years of occupation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
